## TL;DR

The **5 mistakes** buyers make with vintage asscher cut engagement rings:

1. **Buying SI clarity** — step-cut facets act like open mirrors; every inclusion reflects across the whole stone. VS1 minimum, always.
2. **Using a round prong head** — round heads create a circle-on-square tension that kills the geometric look. Use a square or cushion head.
3. **Skipping The Art Deco Mandate** — four elements separate real Art Deco from a modern ring with milgrain dots: milgrain border, geometric side stones, platinum metal, step-cut center.
4. **Missing milgrain** — no milgrain border means no vintage. It is the single non-negotiable marker of the era.
5. **Choosing 14K yellow gold** — authentic 1920s Art Deco was platinum. Yellow gold reads Edwardian (1900–1915), not Jazz Age.

