## Setting Detail Guide — What Each Style Delivers

Not every vintage setting works the same way with an asscher. Here is what to expect from each price tier:

**Entry-level Art Deco ($892–$1,100):** The Fleur-De-Lis and Infinity settings deliver milgrain detail and geometric prong placement at the lowest price point. The trade-off is 14K white gold rather than platinum — rhodium replating every two to three years keeps the bright white color. For buyers on a strict total budget, these are the smart entry point.

**Mid-range pavé ($1,432–$1,987):** The Petite Vintage Leaf, Embossed Vintage, and Milgrain Lace White Gold settings add side stone accents and heavier milgrain borders. These represent the best balance of Art Deco detail and total ring budget. The [Milgrain Lace WG at $1,987](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/milgrain-lace-pave-14k-white-gold-item-315055?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the most complete Art Deco expression in this tier.

**Platinum tier ($1,672–$2,600):** The Enchanted Scroll, Hand-Engraved Micropavé, and Milgrain Lace Platinum settings use the historically correct metal. They never yellow, never need replating, and hold milgrain crisp for decades. Pay more once, maintain nothing.

**Statement settings ($3,199–$7,384):** The Side Stone and Baguette settings shift the ring from delicate to architectural. Five baguette side stones flanking the asscher is the definitive Art Deco statement — geometric diamonds meeting a geometric center at every edge.

