## Clarity and Color for the Step-Cut Standard

Clarity is everything with a step-cut stone — more than with any brilliant-cut shape. The [asscher cut diamond](/asscher-cut-diamond-engagement-rings) has long flat facets that work like open mirrors. There is nowhere for an inclusion to hide.

VS1 is the minimum. That grade means nothing visible to the naked eye at normal viewing distance. Anything below that and you will see it.

**Color for platinum or white gold settings:** E or F is ideal. White metal amplifies color tints, so a G stone in platinum can look slightly warm in natural daylight. D is always correct but rarely necessary — most buyers cannot see the difference between D and F in a set stone.

**Color for yellow or rose gold settings:** G works well because the warm metal tone neutralizes tints equally across all grades. The [Fleur-De-Lis Rose Gold setting](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/art-deco-inspired-fleur-de-lis-pave-14k-rose-gold-item-315297?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) paired with an F or G stone is a well-matched combination.

**Cut grade note:** Blue Nile lists asscher diamonds as Ideal Cut, referring to overall proportioning. GIA does not assign cut grades to step-cut shapes. Look for table percentages of 60–68% and depth percentages of 60–68% — these proportions deliver the deepest Hall of Mirrors reflection.

## Choosing the Right Setting Depth

Vintage settings sit higher off the finger than modern low-profile designs. This was intentional — the architectural silhouette was a design feature of Art Deco style, not a flaw. A tall setting with intricate milgrain work makes the ring look like a piece of architecture on the hand.

The practical consideration: taller settings catch on clothing and hair more easily. Buyers with active work environments or physical jobs do better with the [Embossed Vintage WG](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/embossed-vintage-style-14k-white-gold-item-305209?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) or [Milgrain Lace WG](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/milgrain-lace-pave-14k-white-gold-item-315055?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), which sit lower without losing milgrain detail.

If the lifestyle allows a taller ring, the [Five-Stone Tapered Baguette Platinum](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/five-stone-tapered-baguette-platinum-item-192579?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the most dramatic vintage setting available. Its cascading baguette shoulders are pure 1925 design logic.

## How a Vintage Asscher Ring Photographs — What to Expect

Asscher cut diamonds photograph differently than brilliant cuts. A round brilliant photographs as a burst of white and rainbow sparkle because of its 57 to 58 facets firing in all directions. An asscher photographs as a deep, structured geometric well — the Hall of Mirrors pattern is visible as repeating concentric squares pulling the eye toward the center.

In vintage settings, this effect is amplified. The milgrain border creates a frame around the stone that emphasizes its square shape. Baguette shoulder stones add horizontal lines that reinforce the geometry.

The overall photograph reads as architectural rather than sparkling.

This is not a weakness. It is a different kind of beauty — one that photographs better in natural, indirect light than under a ring photographer's direct spot. In daylight near a window, a vintage asscher ring has a quiet, intense presence.

Under a direct studio light, it may look darker than a brilliant cut ring in a comparable photograph.

If you are choosing this ring partly because of how it will look in photos, test it in both indoor and outdoor light before finalizing. Most online retailers offer 360-degree video and daylight photography — look at both before deciding.

The vintage setting adds one more visual advantage: in photos, the milgrain border catches light and separates the stone from the shank visually. The stone appears to float inside a detailed frame rather than sit on top of a band. This effect is most visible in the Milgrain Lace and Enchanted Scroll settings.

## How to Read a GIA Report for an Asscher Diamond

GIA does not assign cut grades to step-cut shapes — the GIA cut grade you see on round brilliant reports does not exist for asscher cuts. When you open a GIA report for an asscher, focus on these four fields:

**Table percentage:** should be between 60% and 68%. A larger table opens up the Hall of Mirrors effect. A smaller table (under 58%) closes it down and makes the stone look darker.

**Depth percentage:** should be between 60% and 68%. Too shallow means the stone loses its characteristic depth. Too deep means the stone looks smaller face-up than its carat weight implies.

**Inclusions and position:** the GIA clarity report includes a diagram showing where inclusions sit inside the stone. For a step-cut, position matters as much as grade. An inclusion at the center of the table is visible in the mirror reflection. An inclusion near the girdle edge is usually invisible once the stone is set.

**Polish and symmetry:** both should be Excellent or Very Good. These grades are available for step-cuts and affect how crisply the facets meet at their edges — which matters more for an asscher than for a brilliant cut where minor polish flaws disappear in sparkle.

